UNIT stringify
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic

# The DEFAULT export (module.exports) of fast-safe-stringify@2.1.1: the
# insertion-order, cycle-safe serializer. This is NOT the .stable / .stableStringify
# variant (which sorts keys) — do not reconstruct the sorted variant.

SIG stringify(obj: any, replacer?: function | array | null | undefined,
              spacer?: number | string | null | undefined) -> string | undefined
# NOTE: the real source also accepts a 4th `options` arg (depthLimit/edgesLimit).
# It is OUT-OF-SCOPE for this unit (see SCOPE). The blind re-emitter implements a
# 3-arg function. The generator NEVER passes a 4th arg, so the default options
# (depthLimit = Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER, edgesLimit = Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER) are
# always in force and never trigger the '[...]' limit replacement. Treat the unit
# as 3-arg.

DEPENDS-ON none   # zero runtime dependencies; relies only on the JS engine's JSON.stringify

# =============================================================================
BEHAVIOR
# =============================================================================
#
# CORE CONTRACT (the whole point of this unit): for any ACYCLIC input that the
# JS engine's native JSON.stringify can serialize WITHOUT THROWING, this function
# returns EXACTLY JSON.stringify(obj, replacer, spacer) — byte-for-byte identical.
# It is a transparent pass-through to JSON.stringify on the success path; the only
# value-add is cycle handling (out of scope here) and a catch-all on the throw path.
#
# The blind re-emitter SHOULD implement this by literally delegating to the host's
# JSON.stringify with the SAME three arguments, wrapped exactly as nodes B1/B2 below.
# All JSON.stringify semantics (replacer, spacer, value-omission, number specials,
# string escaping, toJSON, key ordering, etc.) are inherited verbatim and are NOT
# re-implemented. The notes B3..B11 below pin down those inherited semantics so the
# re-emitter can SELF-CHECK its delegation is faithful; they are descriptive of
# JSON.stringify, not extra logic to add.
#
# ----- PIPELINE (ordered) -----
#
# B0. Argument intake. Function takes (obj, replacer, spacer). (The real source
#     normalizes a missing 4th `options` arg to defaultOptions(); since this unit
#     never receives a 4th arg, that branch always runs and is a no-op for scope.)
#
# B1. Cycle/limit analysis pass (decirc).
#     The real source first walks `obj` with an internal `decirc` routine to detect
#     circular references and (optionally) depth/edge limits, temporarily mutating
#     the object graph to splice in sentinel strings. For ACYCLIC input within the
#     default (effectively-infinite) limits, decirc finds NOTHING to replace: it
#     performs NO net mutation, leaves its bookkeeping empty, and the object graph
#     is unchanged on entry to B2. The function is also IDEMPOTENT and leaves the
#     input object intact after returning (verified: re-stringifying the same object
#     yields identical output and the object is unmodified). Therefore, FOR THE
#     IN-SCOPE (ACYCLIC) DOMAIN, the re-emitter MAY OMIT decirc entirely and go
#     straight to B2 with the original `obj`.
#     One observable edge survives from B1 even for acyclic input — see B2-THROW-B.
#
# B2. Serialize. Compute:
#         res = JSON.stringify(obj, replacer, spacer)
#     and return `res`.
#     - `replacer` and `spacer` are passed THROUGH UNCHANGED to JSON.stringify
#       (replacerStack is empty in the acyclic/no-getter case, so the source's
#        replaceGetterValues wrapper is NOT applied; plain replacer is used).
#     - If JSON.stringify returns `undefined` (e.g. top-level value is undefined, a
#       function, or a symbol, or the replacer maps the root to undefined), the unit
#       returns `undefined` (NOT the string "undefined"). VERIFIED.
#
# B2-THROW (the ONLY behavioral divergence from raw JSON.stringify in scope):
#     There are two distinct throw situations, distinguished by WHERE the throw
#     originates relative to the try/catch that wraps the JSON.stringify call:
#
#     B2-THROW-A  (CAUGHT -> sentinel string):
#       If JSON.stringify(obj, replacer, spacer) itself THROWS, the throw is caught
#       and the function returns:
#           JSON.stringify('[unable to serialize, circular reference is too complex to analyze]')
#       i.e. the EXACT string (INCLUDING the surrounding double-quotes, because the
#       literal is itself run through JSON.stringify):
#           "[unable to serialize, circular reference is too complex to analyze]"
#       There is no replacer/spacer applied to this fallback.
#       Cases that reach this CAUGHT path (all VERIFIED):
#         * A BigInt appears ANYWHERE in the serialized graph (top-level, object
#           property value, array element, or returned by a configurable getter).
#           Native JSON.stringify throws TypeError "Do not know how to serialize a
#           BigInt"; this unit swallows it and returns the sentinel string. THIS IS
#           THE PRIMARY DIVERGENCE FROM JSON.stringify AND IS IN-CONTRACT.
#         * An object's toJSON() method throws (the throw happens inside
#           JSON.stringify, so it is caught).
#         * A configurable getter that returns a non-serializable value (e.g. a
#           BigInt) — the getter is invoked by JSON.stringify, throw is caught.
#       The exact returned bytes are independent of WHICH such case occurred.
#
#     B2-THROW-B  (NOT caught -> re-thrown, matches JSON.stringify):
#       If the throw originates in the B1 decirc walk — specifically an ENUMERABLE
#       throwing GETTER, because decirc reads `val[key]` to recurse BEFORE the
#       try block — the exception PROPAGATES out of the function unchanged. This
#       matches what raw JSON.stringify would throw for the same input (VERIFIED:
#       both throw Error "getter boom" for an enumerable getter that throws).
#       NOTE: B2-THROW-B is reachable ONLY via getters/property descriptors, which
#       are NOT expressible through the oracle JSON codec, so the generator does not
#       exercise it; it is documented for completeness and listed under OUT.
#       For a re-emitter that simply delegates to JSON.stringify (omitting decirc),
#       this path is naturally satisfied: JSON.stringify itself throws the same error.
#       CAUTION for re-emitters that DO implement decirc: a getter-throw must NOT be
#       caught (it is outside the try); only the JSON.stringify call is wrapped.
#
# ----- INHERITED JSON.stringify SEMANTICS (self-check reference; all VERIFIED) -----
#
# B3. Value omission / null-ization:
#     - Object PROPERTY whose value is undefined, a function, or a symbol: the
#       property is OMITTED.            {a:undefined,b:1} -> {"b":1}
#     - ARRAY ELEMENT that is undefined, a function, or a symbol: serialized as
#       null.                            [undefined,1,()=>{}] -> [null,1,null]
#     - Sparse array holes serialize as null. [1,,3] -> [1,null,3]
#     - null property/element preserved: {a:null} -> {"a":null}
#
# B4. Number specials:
#     - NaN, Infinity, -Infinity serialize as null (as property value OR array
#       element OR top-level). {a:NaN} -> {"a":null}; top-level NaN -> "null".
#     - -0 serializes as 0 (both as value and array element). {a:-0} -> {"a":0}.
#     - Normal number formatting matches the engine: 1e21 -> 1e+21, 1e-7 -> 1e-7,
#       1.0 -> 1, 123456789012345680000 -> 123456789012345680000, 0.1 -> 0.1.
#
# B5. String escaping (engine-native JSON escaping). Examples VERIFIED:
#     - " -> \"   ;  \ -> \\
#     - \b -> \b ; \f -> \f ; \n -> \n ; \r -> \r ; \t -> \t
#     - Control chars U+0000..U+001F not covered above -> \u00XX (lowercase hex,
#       4 digits). e.g. U+0001 -> , U+001F -> .
#     - Printable / non-ASCII Unicode (e.g. é, U+1F600 😀) emitted as the raw
#       UTF-8 character, NOT escaped. Same escaping applies to OBJECT KEYS.
#
# B6. Booleans/null/strings/numbers at top level serialize as their JSON token:
#     42 -> "42", "hi" -> "\"hi\"", true -> "true", null -> "null".
#
# B7. toJSON: if a value (object) has a toJSON() method, JSON.stringify calls it and
#     serializes the RESULT. Date -> ISO string ("1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z").
#     Custom {toJSON(){return {y:1}}} -> {"y":1}. (Native delegation handles this;
#     the default variant does NOT special-case toJSON in B1.) NOTE: a live toJSON
#     METHOD is not expressible through the oracle JSON codec (the method is lost on
#     persistence), so the generator does NOT emit toJSON-bearing objects; this
#     semantic is inherited verbatim from JSON.stringify and needs no separate logic.
#
# B8. Replacer:
#     - As a FUNCTION fn(key,value): called for the synthetic root key "" first,
#       then each key in insertion order, with `this` bound to the holder object;
#       its return value is serialized; returning undefined omits (object) /
#       null-izes (array). Replacer call key sequence for {a:{b:1}} is ["","a","b"]
#       (VERIFIED). The function and spacer compose normally.
#     - As an ARRAY of keys (an allowlist): only those keys (matched as strings)
#       are included, recursively, preserving the object's own key order (NOT the
#       array's order). Numeric keys in the array are coerced to strings ([1] keeps
#       key "1"). Empty array -> "{}" for objects. Array-of-keys replacer applied to
#       an array-of-objects filters each object's keys. (All VERIFIED.)
#     - undefined / null replacer: no transformation (plain JSON.stringify).
#
# B9. Spacer (indentation):
#     - number: min(floor(n),10) spaces of indent per level; n<=0 (incl 0) ->
#       compact (no whitespace). Fractional floored (2.9 -> 2 spaces). n>10 clamps
#       to 10. (VERIFIED: 12 -> 10 spaces.)
#     - string: used verbatim as the indent unit, truncated to its first 10
#       characters if longer. Empty string -> compact. (VERIFIED: "0123456789ABCDEF"
#       -> "0123456789".)
#     - array spacer (or any other non-number/non-string): treated as no spacer ->
#       compact output. (VERIFIED: [1,2] -> compact.)
#     - undefined / null spacer: compact.
#
# B10. Key ordering (insertion-order variant): object keys serialized in JS own-key
#      enumeration order — array-index-like integer keys first in ascending numeric
#      order, then string keys in insertion order. {"2","1","10","x"} ->
#      {"1","2","10","x"} (VERIFIED). This unit does NOT sort keys (that is .stable).
#
# B11. Statelessness: the function holds no observable cross-call state for in-scope
#      inputs (a BigInt call followed by a normal call yields the normal result;
#      the input object is never left mutated). VERIFIED.

ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# Deterministic, pure. For each generated [obj, replacer?, spacer?] tuple the oracle
# records the returned string (or `undefined`, or — for inputs that drive B2-THROW-A,
# e.g. BigInt — the exact sentinel string "\"[unable to serialize, circular reference
# is too complex to analyze]\""). No seam: JSON.stringify is a pure host primitive,
# not an ambient source, so no injection is required.

# =============================================================================
SCOPE
# =============================================================================
#
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly):
#   - Delegation to JSON.stringify(obj, replacer, spacer) on the acyclic success
#     path (nodes B1-acyclic-noop, B2). All inherited semantics B3..B11.
#   - The B2-THROW-A catch behavior: any JSON.stringify throw on an in-scope input
#     -> return the EXACT string  "[unable to serialize, circular reference is too
#     complex to analyze]"  (quote-wrapped, via JSON.stringify of the literal).
#     This is the only divergence from raw JSON.stringify in scope. PRIMARY trigger
#     in the generated corpus: BigInt anywhere in the graph.
#   - Returning the host `undefined` (not a string) when JSON.stringify yields
#     undefined.
#
# CARRIED DATA: none. The only embedded literals are two short sentinel STRINGS
#   ('[Circular]' = CIRCULAR_REPLACE_NODE, '[...]' = LIMIT_REPLACE_NODE) and the
#   fallback message '[unable to serialize, circular reference is too complex to
#   analyze]'. '[Circular]' and '[...]' belong to OUT-OF-SCOPE paths (cycles /
#   limits) and are unreachable here. The fallback message IS reachable (B2-THROW-A)
#   and its exact text is transcribed inline in BEHAVIOR above — it is a single short
#   string, not a table, so NO carried.json is needed.
#
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-SCOPE (documented; NOT generated, NOT graded):
#   - CIRCULAR REFERENCES -> '[Circular]'. The headline feature of the package, but
#     the oracle JSON codec has NO cyclic tag, so a cycle cannot be expressed as an
#     input value. Cannot be round-tripped -> OUT. (Behavior: each back-edge to an
#     ancestor on the stack is replaced by the string "[Circular]"; the original
#     graph is restored after serialization.)
#   - depthLimit / edgesLimit options (the 4th `options` arg) and the '[...]'
#     LIMIT_REPLACE_NODE sentinel. The unit is treated as 3-arg; default limits are
#     effectively infinite and never fire. OUT.
#   - The .stable / .stableStringify (sorted-key) sibling export. OUT — different unit.
#   - B2-THROW-B (enumerable throwing getter -> re-throw): reachable only via getters
#     / property descriptors, which the JSON codec cannot express. Documented above;
#     OUT for generation. A delegating re-emitter satisfies it automatically.
#   - NON-CODEC-FAITHFUL values (must NOT be emitted by genInputs; behavior still
#     specified for a blind re-emitter):
#       * Symbol values: omitted (object prop) / null (array elem) — same as
#         undefined. Symbols don't round-trip the codec -> OUT for generation.
#       * Functions-as-data: omitted (object prop) / null (array elem). Functions
#         don't round-trip the codec -> OUT for generation (behavior identical to
#         undefined regardless).
#       * undefined as a VALUE (object prop omitted / array elem -> null): does not
#         round-trip the codec -> OUT for generation; behavior specified in B3.
#       * Boxed primitives (new Number/new String/new Boolean): unwrapped to their
#         primitive by JSON.stringify ({a:new Number(5)} -> {"a":5}). Don't round-trip
#         the codec -> OUT for generation.
#       * NaN / +-Infinity / -0 as RAW numbers: do not survive the JSON codec
#         round-trip, so the generator does NOT emit them. Their behavior is fully
#         specified in B4 (NaN/+-Infinity -> null; -0 -> 0) so a blind re-emitter
#         reconstructs it; they are simply not in the corpus.
#       * Objects with a live toJSON() METHOD: the method does not survive
#         JSON.stringify(encode(args)) persistence (it is invoked/dropped, leaving a
#         plain object), so a toJSON-bearing input would poison the stored expected.
#         NOT emitted. Behavior (B7) is inherited from JSON.stringify and specified.
#       * Date values: serialize via toJSON to an ISO string (B7) but the codec does
#         not reconstruct a Date instance -> NOT emitted as data.

PROVENANCE fast-safe-stringify@2.1.1 ; repo github.com/davidmarkclements/fast-safe-stringify ;
           license MIT ; source file index.js (module.exports = stringify ; the
           DEFAULT export, lines 1-105 + the JSON.stringify call + catch at 20-46).

# =============================================================================
RE-EMITTER DIRECTIVE (this unit is the DEFAULT export)
# =============================================================================
# The reconstruction module MUST `export default <fn>` where <fn> is the stringify
# function — the harness imports the DEFAULT export and calls it as fn(obj, replacer,
# spacer). Simplest faithful reconstruction:
#
#   export default function stringify (obj, replacer, spacer) {
#     try {
#       return JSON.stringify(obj, replacer, spacer)
#     } catch (_) {
#       return JSON.stringify('[unable to serialize, circular reference is too complex to analyze]')
#     }
#   }
#
# (Cycle handling is omitted because no in-scope input contains a cycle; the catch
# reproduces B2-THROW-A; an in-decirc getter-throw is naturally re-thrown by
# JSON.stringify itself, satisfying B2-THROW-B. This is behaviorally equivalent to
# the original ON THE IN-SCOPE DOMAIN.)
